    "_level_note": "`level` records the Class I / Class II distinction (Siegel 1974; Kiparsky 1982). Class I suffixes (-ic, -al, -ity, -ous) attach CLOSER to the root and can shift stress; Class II (-ly, -ness, -ful, -less) attach outside them and leave stress alone. That ordering is why hypothermically is therm+ic+al+ly and never therm+ly+ic+al. Treat it as a strong tendency, not a law: Fabb (1988) showed level ordering overpredicts, and Hay & Plag (2004) replace it with complexity-based ordering, where a suffix that is easier to parse out attaches outside one that is harder - gradient, and computable from a corpus."
  },
